<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kel Ahaggar</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1977 CE (approx.) · capital Tamanrasset</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Tuareg confederation of the Hoggar mountains in southern Algeria, led by an amenokal and among the last Saharan peoples to submit to France.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Ahaggar</a> took shape as a confederation in the Ahaggar massif of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, with noble drum groups, especially the Kel Rela, providing the amenokal. Its organisation in recognisable form dates from around the mid-18th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Noble warriors controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> goat-herding tribes and cultivators of small oases, often of servile status. The Kel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaggar-mountains">Ahaggar</a> escorted and raided caravans, collected tribute and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kel-ajjer">Kel Ajjer</a> in the 1870s. In 1881 they destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flatters-expedition">Flatters expedition</a> surveying a trans-Saharan railway.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After their defeat by French forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tit">Tit</a> in 1902, the amenokal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moussa-ag-amastan">Moussa ag Amastan</a> accepted French rule and befriended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-foucauld">Charles de Foucauld</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamanrasset">Tamanrasset</a>. The confederation survived under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria</a> and independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, where the traditional office of amenokal lost its role in the 1970s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kel-ahaggar.jpg" alt="Kel Ahaggar" width="320" height="186"><figcaption>Image: بلهواري محمد فيصل · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kel_Ahaggar_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1750 CE – 1977 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tamanrasset</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Moussa ag Amastan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tamahaq (Tuareg)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kel Ahaggar confederation, Hoggar Tuareg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1864&amp;focus=kel-ahaggar" aria-label="Kel Ahaggar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4828 1337 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kel Ahaggar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5148 1593L5077 1108"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5077 1108h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5148 1593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5148 1593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tamanrasset. <a href="/atlas?year=1864&amp;focus=kel-ahaggar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

